# repro.log — puma-cve-2020-5249  (CVE-2020-5247 -> CVE-2020-5249)
# HTTP Response Splitting / CRLF header injection (CWE-113), Puma (rubygem).
# generated: 2026-07-13

## Roles / versions (built from immutable git TAGS — puma 4.3.2 & 3.12.3 are YANKED from rubygems)
  V0 = puma v4.3.1  (port 39331) — pre CVE-2020-5247, both sinks vulnerable
  V1 = puma v4.3.2  (port 39332) — CVE-2020-5247 patched = TARGET; regular-header sink
                                   guarded by `headers.reject! { CRLF_REGEX =~ v.to_s }`,
                                   early-hints lambda UNCHECKED  <- the residual
  V2 = puma v4.3.3  (port 39333) — CVE-2020-5249 patched; `possible_header_injection?`
                                   applied to ALL sinks incl. early hints

## Guard evolution (lib/puma/server.rb + const.rb; verbatim — see patch.diff)
  const.rb  v4.3.2:  CRLF_REGEX           = /[\r\n]/.freeze
  const.rb  v4.3.3:  HTTP_INJECTION_REGEX = /[\r\n]/.freeze     # SAME pattern; renamed
  server.rb v4.3.2 (CVE-2020-5247, incomplete): after @app.call, on the MAIN headers only:
        headers.reject! { |_k, v| CRLF_REGEX =~ v.to_s }
     -> the env[EARLY_HINTS] lambda has NO check.
  server.rb v4.3.3 (CVE-2020-5249, complete): reject! removed; instead
        next if possible_header_injection?(v)     # inside early-hints lambda   (residual closed)
        next if possible_header_injection?(vs)    # content-length branch
        next if possible_header_injection?(v)     # main value-emit loop
   => The incompleteness is the MISSED SINK (early hints), NOT a missed character.
      Both fixes reject the SAME char class /[\r\n]/.

## Faithful-core
  ruby:2.7-slim + `bundle install` puma from git tag; tiny Rack app (config.ru):
    ?x=<v>   -> regular response header  X-Reflected: <v>   (CVE-2020-5247 sink)
    ?eh=<v>  -> early-hints header        Link: <v>          (CVE-2020-5249 residual sink)
  puma_config.rb: `early_hints true` enables env['rack.early_hints'].

## Payload (identical for seed & residual; ONLY the sink/param differs)
  value = "pcbseed\rX-Pcb-Injected: <FLAG>"     (lone CR \r, URL-encoded in the query)
  Raw-socket client reads the exact response bytes; FLAG in raw = Puma accepted the
  split-inducing header value. (The request path is NOT part of the flag-detection
  surface — only the server's response bytes are.)

## Raw response evidence (illustration flag = PCBFLAG_debugtoken123456)
